{"name":"Birth of Venus","hex":"#f6ebc2","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"birth","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process of childbearing; the beginning of life; the emergence of a human baby or other viviparous animal offspring from the mother's body into the environment.","An instance of childbirth."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["A familial relationship established by childbirth."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/birth"},{"term":"venus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the bivalve molluscs in the genus Venus or family Veneridae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/venus"}],"entries":[{"title":"The Birth of Venus","blocks":[{"text":"The Birth of Venus is a painting by the Italian artist Sandro Botticelli, probably executed in the mid-1480s. It depicts the goddess Venus arriving at the shore after her birth, when she had emerged from the sea fully grown. The painting is in the Uffizi Gallery in Florence, Italy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/Sandro_Botticelli_-_La_nascita_di_Venere_-_Google_Art_Project_-_edited.jpg/330px-Sandro_Botticelli_-_La_nascita_di_Venere_-_Google_Art_Project_-_edited.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Birth_of_Venus","wikidata":"Q151047"},{"title":"Birth","blocks":[{"text":"Birth is the act or process of bearing or bringing forth offspring, also referred to in technical contexts as parturition. In mammals, the process is initiated by hormones which cause the muscular walls of the uterus to contract, expelling the fetus at a developmental stage when it is ready to feed and breathe."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Lambing_in_England_-10March2012_%282%29.jpg/330px-Lambing_in_England_-10March2012_%282%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Birth","wikidata":"Q14819852"},{"title":"Venus","blocks":[{"text":"Venus is the second planet from the Sun. Similar in size and mass to Earth, Venus has no liquid water, and its atmosphere is far thicker and denser than that of any other rocky body in the Solar System. The atmosphere is composed mostly of carbon dioxide and has a thick cloud layer of sulfuric acid that spans the whole planet. At the mean surface level, the atmosphere reaches a temperature of 737 K, making it the hottest planet in the solar system and also a pressure 92 times greater than Earth's at sea level, turning the lowest layer of the atmosphere into a supercritical fluid. From Earth, Venus is visible as a star-like point of light, appearing brighter than any other natural point of light in the sky, as either the brightest \"morning star\" or \"evening star\". It was also given the poetic name Ēarendel in Old English, meaning \"ray of light\" along with the two previous epithets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Venus_from_Mariner_10.jpg/330px-Venus_from_Mariner_10.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus","wikidata":"Q313"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Venus_botticelli_detail.jpg/500px-Venus_botticelli_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Venus_botticelli_detail.jpg/1280px-Venus_botticelli_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Venus_botticelli_detail.jpg/500px-Venus_botticelli_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Detail: the face of Venus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVenus_botticelli_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wik